f you might say agency going  from the master to the slave and also to  the independent thing that has worked  upon and then there's this circular  current running through them and that  represents the developing  self-consciousness of the slave as an  independent consciousness no longer  merely dependent no longer merely a  reflection of the will of the master but  something that has been attained  something that has been achieved and you  notice I've got three things picked out  here fear service and work each of these  Hegel stresses plays a key role if you  have one of these without having the  others you don't really get the sort of  development that Hagel is taking place  and so for example he will tell us if  you don't have the right sort of fear  all you end up with is a kind of  stubbornness or or self-centeredness on  the part of the servant and not  something that is transformative and  leading to to a new type of  consciousness  so let's actually take a look at some of  the things that he says I'm going to go  through this text fairly quickly and and  not you know touching on every single  part but he says what we've got here is  is a servant or a slave who exists in  relation to the Lord or master that is  their essential reality and so the Eagle  talks here about the slave discovering  the truth of themselves and at first  they take the truth to lie in the master  the master is really the one giving the  truth to them as what they are  something like as Aristotle called it an  unsold tool going to work on objects and  transforming them for the enjoyment of  the master but something else is taking  place here so Hegel first talks about  the negativity that is involved on the  part of the the slave the negativity is  not just that they feel bad emotions or  that they they're deprived of you know  the fruits of their labor but the very  capacity for being otherwise is which is  essential to self consciousness is that  negativity there's negativity upon  negativity involved in the life and the  consciousness of the slave and that is  essential to what it means for Hegel to  be human being so he goes on and he says  one way in which this is experienced is  through fear he says this consciousness  has been fearful not of this or that  particular thing or just at odd moments  which could apply to the master - right  but its whole being has been seized with  dread it has experienced the fear of  death the absolute Lord the absolute  master who even the master  is subject to and here you said  something really important in this  experience it has been shaken to the  very core of its existence it is not  just felt a feeling it has had the  entire meaning of its world and of  itself and its existence called into  question and you know perhaps even  dangled in front of its face is  something that's being taken away from  it knowing that it could die at any  moment if the master or a masters kid or  you know some other master or some other  contingent circumstance decide so this  level of fear this extent of fear Hegel  says is necessary in order for this  transformation to take place so he says  what what's going on here this is a  universal movement an absolute melting  away of everything stable that reveals  to us the is he says essential nature of  self consciousness absolute negativity  pure being for self now he says this  becomes explicit for the the slave for  the bondsman for the Lord or the master  it exists for him as his object now is  this enough to simply feel fear to  produce the transformation and  self-consciousness that Hegel is talking  about here no these two other elements  are needed service and work so Hegel  will go on and tell us that this fear is  only implicitly this dissolution and  what we need here is service service of  who service of the Lord it provides a  certain sort of stability to the  instability that the fear he has he says  the fear of the Lord is the beginning of  wisdom but consciousness is not there in  aware that it is a being for self it's  not fully self aware through work he  says the slave becomes conscious of what  he truly is so the truth of the slave at  this point begins to no longer  lie in the master but in the work in the  thing the master is a consciousness who  sets the slave to work by making them  fear by giving them determinate things  to work upon the slave does service for  the master but the slave does that  precisely by turning to something else  the independent thing that has to be  worked upon whether it is mopping the  floor or doing the taxes or dancing in  which case it's your own body that is  the independent thing that you're taking  control over there's a subordination not  only to the master in service but to the  thing itself  Hegel goes on aney and he says that  desire has reserved to itself the pure  negating of the object thereby it's  unalloyed feeling of self the slave is  forced to put desire on hold  they cannot satisfy themselves think of  farming for example or even better since  this is something that we can all relate  to I think a little bit easier I think  about cooking when you're cooking for  somebody else do you get to sample the  dish as you're going through well you  may in fact taste the sauce a little bit  just to make sure everything is on track  but if you're making a nice little  cutlet for somebody you don't cut off a  little portion and pop it in your mouth  you you know if they're in the position  of the master and you're making  something for them they get the good  stuff and you'll get to taste it later  on  or perhaps you add it earlier in if  you're working at a restaurant with the  family meal or the crew meal beforehand  right but you have to put your desire on  hold anybody who's worked in restaurant  settings knows exactly what I'm talking  about you put your desire on hold for  hours and hours a very basic desire to  taste to touch to enjoy that is for the  patrons so work he says the desire held  in check fleeting the staved off work  forms and che  the thing that is what is required if  you want to form and shape the thing you  can't be enjoying the thing and by doing  this self conscious the self  consciousness of the slave is something  radically different than that of the  master it is getting to know itself  through the world of objects so Hegel  goes on and he says this is the negative  middle term or formative activity right  and he says in this way consciousness  qua worker comes to see in the  independent being of the object its own  independence its own independence  through working now Hegel towards the  end tells us he makes the explicit  connection that you have to have all  three of these he says you know we have  the the first moment the negative  significance of fear and then we have  service and then we have work and he  says in fashioning the thing the  bondsman's own negativity his being for  self becomes an object form only through  his setting at not the existing shape  confronting him but this objective  negative moment is none other than the  alien being before whom it is trembled  it looks at the thing as sort of an  extension of the master and it makes  itself nothing in in relation to it and  then he says now he destroys this alien  negative moment posits himself as a  negative in the permanent order of  things and becomes for himself somebody  existing at his own account in turning  to the object that I'm doing I'm working  upon because the master put me to do it  and I'm serving the master and I'm  afraid of the master by working on the  object I come to realize that the master  doesn't have the power that I took them  to have a total power over everything  they are restricted they don't actually  know the object I know the object  because I work on it and I transform it  and I am a slave in that case in a  certain sense greater than the master I  develop a consciousness of myself  through  doing this as I said 